  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 1157 AM MDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nStarting Sunday, an upper level ridge starts to amplify in the \nCentral Plains and into Montana as a low-pressure system in the \nNorthwest slides northeast into Southern Canada on Monday. Once \nthe low-pressure passes through, southwesterly flow returns and \nwill stay until a broad upper-level trough comes through from \nthe north, eventually returning back into the ridge pattern late\nin the week. This will allow chances for showers and cooling \ntemperatures over next week. Monday and Tuesday will have the \nfirst potential for showers as the cold front moves through and \ntransitions into southwesterly flow with embedded shortwaves on \ntuesday. There is high confidence (80%) that the cold front will\noccur Monday afternoon, but there is medium confidence (50%) \nthat showers will linger into Tuesday. \n\nOn Wednesday and Thursday, conditions for showers look to be \nsimilar to Monday and Tuesday. A broader cold front will pass \nthrough overnight Wednesday into Thursday morning bringing a \ndrier air mass and a ridge pattern over the area. Confidence \ndecreases from medium to low (50-20%) over the two days as there\nis a large variability on when the cold front will pass \nthrough. While there is a decent chance (up to 45%) for showers \nacross the area, latest guidance favors the southern half of the\narea for accumulations greater than a tenth of an inch of rain \nbased on the southerly motion and timing of the front. \n\nTemperatures will start in the low 90s Sunday and dip into the \nupper 80s and low 90s Monday. For Tuesday onwards, afternoon \nhighs should remain in the 70s as a 5-degree temperature spread \nstarts to form over the forecast area that could potentially put\nhighs into the upper 70s. There is medium confidence (60%) that\ntemperature will rise later in the week as we move back into \nthe ridge pattern.",
